<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gerald Durrell</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gerald Durrell was a British naturalist, writer and conservationist. He led animal-collecting expeditions to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, British Guiana and elsewhere and described them in popular books such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-bafut-beagles">The Bafut Beagles</a>, while My Family and Other Animals told of his childhood on Corfu. In 1959 he founded Jersey Zoo, which pioneered the captive breeding of endangered species.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gerald-durrell.jpg" alt="Gerald Durrell" width="249" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Byron Patchett · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gerald_Durrell,_Askania_Nova_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gerald Malcolm Durrell was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamshedpur">Jamshedpur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> on 7 January 1925, the youngest of the children of Lawrence Samuel Durrell, a civil engineer, and Louisa Florence Durrell, née Dixie. The family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> after his father died in 1928. In March 1935 they left for the Greek island of Corfu and stayed four years, until the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> forced them back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><p>Back in Bournemouth by early 1940 he was taught by a tutor, Harold Binns, a veteran of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, who gave him a love of poetry and good writing. He spent the next two years exploring the countryside and reading books from the local library.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1946 Durrell received an inheritance from his father's will, and he used it to fund collecting trips. He visited the British Cameroons in 1947 and 1948 with the ornithologist John Yealland, returned in 1949 with Ken Smith, and went to British Guiana in 1950. These trips cost more than they earned. He married Jacquie in 1951, and she persuaded him to write down his stories. After a radio talk on his search for the hairy frog he wrote The Overloaded Ark, which sold well.</p><p>An expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> followed in 1953 and produced The Drunken Forest. My Family and Other Animals became a bestseller. He planned it meticulously in three parts, one for each of the family's villas, and wrote 120,000 words in six weeks. The Amateur Naturalist, written with his second wife Lee, became his most successful book, selling well over a million copies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Durrell wanted his own zoo, one meant for the study and captive breeding of animals rather than as a showcase. Hugh Fraser, the owner of Les Augrès manor on Jersey, agreed to a lease signed on 17 October 1958. The zoo opened on 26 March 1959, and in 1963 its control passed to the Jersey Wildlife Preservation Trust. An appeal in 1970 raised funds to buy the property.</p><p>In 1984 he founded the Durrell Conservation Academy to train people in captive breeding. He was diagnosed with liver cancer and cirrhosis in 1994, had a liver transplant, and died on 30 January 1995. His ashes were divided between Corfu and Jersey Zoo. Princess Anne, the Trust's patron, said that his ability to put his enthusiasm for wildlife on the page was his most important talent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gerald Durrell” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Fon still reigns; Achirimbi II became widely known through <b>Gerald Durrell's</b> book The Bafut Beagles.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bafut">Kingdom of Bafut</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamshedpur">Jamshedpur</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-helier">Saint Helier</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.8,86.18&amp;year=1970" aria-label="Jamshedpur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gerald Durrell"><image href="/maps/globes/g090n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4699 5609h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4699 5609h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.8,86.18&amp;year=1970">What was here in 1970? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
